.TH AXCALL 1 "14 September 2026" "axcall" "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
axcall \- make an AX.25 connection over KISS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axcall
.RI [ options ]
.I port destination
.br
.B axcall
.RI [ options ]
.B \-\-listen
.I port
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axcall
is a connected-mode AX.25 terminal. It dials a remote station, pipes your
keyboard to it and its replies to your screen, and disconnects cleanly. As is
normal in AX.25 it runs entirely in line mode, and CR/LF translation is done
transparently in both directions. It is 8 bit clean and always UTF\-8.
.PP
It is not the
.B axcall
that came with ax25\-apps. That one opened an
.B AF_AX25
socket on the Linux kernel AX.25 stack, which has since been withdrawn. This one
carries its own AX.25 v2.2 data link and speaks KISS to a TNC over a serial port
or over TCP, so it needs no kernel support,
.BR kissattach (8)
or
.BR axparms (8),
and no privilege. The command line is deliberately the old one, so existing
scripts and existing habits mostly carry over. See
.B COMPATIBILITY
for where they do not.
.PP
The program provides no terminal emulation, no file transfer and no
.RB ` ~ '
escapes. It is a pipe with a link layer under it.
.SH PORT
The
.I port
argument says which TNC to talk to. It is one of three things, told apart by
shape before anything is opened:
.TP
.I name
A name from the ports file, for example
.BR radio .
See
.BR "PORTS FILE" .
.TP
.IR /dev/ttyUSB0 " or " /dev/ttyUSB0:57600
A serial device, optionally with the baud rate. Anything starting with
.RB ` / ',
.RB ` ./ '
or
.RB ` ../ '
is taken as a path.
.TP
.I 10.45.0.66:8001
A KISS\-over\-TCP endpoint. A bracketed IPv6 literal such as
.I [::1]:8001
works too.
.PP
.B \-\-serial
and
.B \-\-tcp
give the same two transports explicitly. When either is used the single
positional argument is the
.IR destination .
.SH OPTIONS
These are spelled as the kernel AX.25
.B axcall
spelled them.
.TP
.BI \-s " mycall"
Connect using the specified source callsign. Required unless the port names one.
.TP
.BI \-p " paclen"
The largest amount of data to send in each frame, N1 (PACLEN). 1 to 1024,
default 256. XID may lower it to the peer's. The kernel version stopped at 500.
.TP
.BI \-w " window"
The send window, k (MAXFRAME): frames in flight before an acknowledgement is
needed. 1 to 7, or 1 to 127 with
.BR "\-m e" .
The default is 4 on either modulus, after which XID takes the lower of what each
end offers. With SREJ negotiated the window is held to half the modulus, so 5 to
7 take effect only on a go\-back\-N link.
.TP
.BI \-m " s|e"
The AX.25 mode to use:
.B s
for normal (modulo 8) operation, the default, and
.B e
for extended (modulo 128, AX.25 v2.2). An extended dial falls back to modulo 8
if the peer answers FRMR or DM, so the call still goes through. Inbound sessions
use whichever the caller asks for.
.TP
.BI \-b " l|e"
The method of backoff. Accepted and ignored: this axcall adapts T1 from the
measured round trip and has no linear/exponential selector. The argument is
still checked, so a stray
.B \-b 9600
is reported rather than passing quietly.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-t ", " \-R ", " \-8
Raw mode, talk mode, no remote commands, and UTF\-8. All four are accepted and
ignored: axcall is always raw and always UTF\-8, and has no remote commands
(#BIN#, 7plus, //echo) to disable.
.TP
.B \-v
Display the version, including the versions of the SDL spec tables and the
runtime libraries it was built against.
.TP
.B \-h
Show a usage summary. Note that the kernel version's
.B \-h
selects slave mode; see
.BR COMPATIBILITY .
.PP
The options below have no counterpart in the kernel version, and are spelled
long so they cannot collide with the letters above.
.TP
.BI \-\-serial " device[:baud]"
Use this serial device, instead of naming it as the
.I port
argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-tcp " host:port"
Use KISS over TCP, instead of naming it as the
.I port
argument.
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " rate"
Serial baud rate, default 57600. Overrides a
.RB ` :baud '
suffix. Ignored for a TCP port.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-listen
Wait for an inbound connection instead of calling out. The kernel stack left
this to
.BR ax25d (8).
.TP
.B \-\-mod128
The same as
.BR "\-m e" .
.TP
.BI \-\-keepalive " seconds"
The idle\-link poll interval, T3, default 300. With no traffic for this long
axcall sends an RR poll to check the link is still up. Must be a positive whole
number: the link layer has no "never poll" setting, so a zero timer would poll
continuously. This
.I keeps a link up
and is not the kernel version's
.BR \-T ,
which drops one.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " n"
Retries before the link is dropped, N2 (RETRY). 1 to 255, default 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-frack " seconds"
The initial acknowledgement timeout, T1 (FRACK). 0.5 to 60, decimals allowed,
default 6. Only the starting point: T1 is adapted from the measured round trip
after the first exchanges.
.TP
.BI \-\-ack\-delay " seconds"
The acknowledgement delay, T2 (RESPTIME). 0 to 30, decimals allowed, default 3.
Received frames are acknowledged together once this has elapsed; 0 acknowledges
every frame at once.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-xid
Skip the XID exchange sent before the SABM on a modulo\-8 dial. Without it axcall
offers SREJ (selective retransmit) and its window; with it the link is plain
go\-back\-N. Outbound only; an accepted
.B \-m e
dial negotiates XID after the UA regardless.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The same as
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-help
The same as
.BR \-h .
.SH PORTS FILE
The ports file binds a short name to a transport, a callsign and optional link
defaults, so that
.B axcall radio gb7rdg
works with nothing else on the line. It is the replacement for
.BR axports (5),
which named a callsign, a paclen and a window but no device, because
.BR kissattach (8)
bound the tty to the port name separately.
.PP
Columns are whitespace\-separated. The first three are mandatory. A
.RB ` \- '
means "not set" in any of the optional ones, and anything after the window
column is a description that is read by people and ignored by axcall. Blank
lines and lines beginning with
.RB ` # '
are skipped.
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
# name   callsign   transport            paclen  window  description
radio    M0LTE\-7    /dev/ttyUSB0:57600   256     4       144.800 MHz
node     M0LTE\-7    10.45.0.66:8001      \-       \-       LinBPQ
.EE
.in
.PP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
is read first and
.I ~/.config/axcall/ports
second, the second replacing same\-named entries from the first. A malformed line
fails the whole load rather than being skipped, so a typo is reported instead of
quietly becoming "unknown port".
.PP
Precedence throughout is that a command\-line flag beats the ports file, which
beats the built\-in default.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ran and the link was closed.
.TP
.B 1
A fatal error.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: the command line, or the ports file.
.TP
.B 3
The modem could not be opened: no such serial device, permission denied, or the
TCP connection was refused.
.TP
.B 4
The connect was refused by the peer (DM) or timed out.
.PP
Note that the kernel version exits 0 even when the connect fails, so a script
that silently tolerated a dead link will start reporting one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AXCALL_PORTS
When set, names the single ports file to read, instead of the two default paths.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/axcall/ports
System ports file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/axcall/ports
Per\-user ports file, overriding same\-named entries in the system one.
.RB ( $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is honoured.)
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The command line is the kernel AX.25
.BR axcall 's,
with these differences.
.PP
.B \-h
means
.BR \-\-help ,
not slave mode. Slave mode was one of three curses screen modes that do not
exist here, and
.B \-h
for help is near\-universal. On its own it prints the usage summary; alongside
other arguments it is a usage error, because printing help and exiting 0 would
look to a ported script like a successful call.
.PP
.BR \-T ", " \-W ", " \-S " and " \-d
are recognised but not yet implemented, and say so.
.PP
.B \-i
(IBM850) is refused. Ignoring it would produce mojibake rather than nothing.
.PP
A digipeater path after the
.IR destination ,
with or without the literal
.BR via ,
is recognised but not yet supported, and is refused rather than ignored.
.PP
NET/ROM and Rose are not supported. The kernel version picked the address family
by trying the port name against
.BR axports (5),
then
.BR nrports (5),
then
.BR rsports (5),
and the same binary was installed as
.B netromcall
and
.BR rosecall .
.PP
There is no ASCII, YAPP, YAPP\-C or 7plus file transfer, no
.RB ` ~ '
escape commands, no status line and no menu bar.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR axlisten (1),
.BR ax25 (4)
.PP
